<p>The app may stay in a "Queued" state for 10-15 minutes in the event that there are no compute resources already available to run your session. Please be patient as additional compute resources are allocated to your session.</p>

<%#
  Slurm-enabled courses only. Every sub-app shares this file, so the guard is
  what keeps the note off the card for courses that have not opted in.

  defined?(context) is belt and braces: if this template is ever rendered
  without a session context, the block is skipped and the card is unchanged.

  The course folder is derived from r_libpath rather than carried as its own
  attribute - r_libpath is <course folder>/R/<arch>-library/<version>, so the
  part before /R/ is the folder and its last segment is the Canvas id.
-%>
<% if defined?(context) && context.respond_to?(:slurm_enabled) && context.slurm_enabled.to_s == "true" %>
  <% course  = context.respond_to?(:r_libpath) ? context.r_libpath.to_s[%r{\A(.*?)/R/}, 1] : nil %>
  <% canvas  = course.to_s.split("/").last %>
  <% if !canvas.to_s.empty? %>
    <p><strong>Running an R script as a batch job.</strong> Copy the class script into a folder of
    your own and submit it. It runs your file with this same image and the same packages, so a job
    produces what you see in the console:</p>

    <pre><code>cp ~/<%= canvas %>/job-tools/run-r-job.sh .
sbatch run-r-job.sh my_script.R</code></pre>

    <p>You do not have to edit the script to ask for more CPUs or time. The full instructions are in
    <code>~/<%= canvas %>/job-tools/README.md</code>.</p>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
